and our shoutout tonight for a  journalist working for Mother Jones  magazine who went undercover in a  potentially very dangerous place inside  a private for-profit prison a place that  this worked hard to keep journalists and  the public from knowing exactly what's  going on inside their walls the  assignment was to get hired as a guard  inside this privately run prison known  as the wind Correctional Center in  Winfield Louisiana so you know I'm gonna  be in there for eight hours a day and  then soon after that I'm gonna be in  there for 12 hours a day  this prison is crazy beyond anything I  ever imagined 1,500 inmates overseen by  guards making $9 an hour mother jones  reporter Shane Bauer was quickly hired  my my first day of training he's the  head of training said to us that at CCA  we'll hire anybody she said you know  people people say say that we're  scraping the bottom of the barrel but  we're not but if you have a driver's  license and are breathing and are  willing to work then we're willing to  hire you  [Music]  get out if you really care about  yourself expose potential inside a place  of chaos and violence all captured on  video by Bauer with a camera hidden in  his watch that he smuggled inside I was  surprised with how kind of bare-bones  the operation was you know there was  very little staff there were you know  maybe twenty five guards for 1,500  prisoners which kind of leads the prison  to be more violence than other prisons  in the state there was an escape while I  was there in the middle of the day he  went up to finish got on roof of the  building the prison staff didn't  actually know that he escaped for  two-three hours not enough people for  security and Bauer says the prison  company cut corners on medical care for  the prisoners by actually my first and  the president met a man who prisoner who  had lost his legs and fingers to  gangrene Bauer says the company is slow  to send anyone to an outside hospital  because it has to pay the bill what kind  of picture picture what you took here  don't belong be yours cover was finally  blown when a photographer for Mother  Jones James West was spotted outside the  prison taking exterior shots at  sheriff's department deputies demanded  to see what was on his camera I think  the best time to seize his camera I will  take it we found out later that someone  at the Sheriff's Department had searched  James's video without a warrant and on  that video was an interview that he had  done with me that day talking about  working in the prison so they were able  to kind of connect that that I was you  know journalists  were quickly moved out calling in his  resignation to the prison company I'm  actually calling because I decided to  resign Bauer's stories in Mother Jones  and a subsequent book got the attention  of the US Department of Justice and  shortly afterwards the Obama  administration announced that they were  going to stop using private prisons on  the federal level this of course is  reversed as soon as Donald Trump became  president so now it's kind of you know  it's back to where it was before all the  more reason that Bauer's undercover  reporting is now so important yeah I  mean we have a rich tradition of  undercover journalism in this country  and it's only in the last couple decades  that we haven't seen it as much and I  think that we have a right to see you  know when companies are serving as  public institutions what they're doing  and if they're not going to allow us  access then we have to figure out how to  get it in a statement the company that  ran that prison at the time now known as  core civic said Bauer's conduct was  reckless could have jeopardized safety  and security and that he obtained  employment under false pretenses but  Ana's application Bauer did describe  himself as a writer and use the name of  the mother Joran's parent company as his  employer so that seems very much on the  up-and-up most telling the statement did  not address in any way the appalling  conditions Bauer discovered inside the  prison which is no longer owned by that  same company so our shout out tonight  for Shane Bauer and Mother Jones for  allowing Americans to see what happens  with important public services and  functions are turned over to companies  whose first priority is to make a profit  it's not a pretty fixture that's our  program for tonight thanks so much for  joining us  we'll see you back here again next week  good night
 

